I’ve reviewed the COG pictures and the WebGLTileLayer code. What’s happening is that, because you never set a layer-extent on the WebGLTileLayer, the renderer happily fetches and draws ANY tile that overlaps the *map* extent.  Tiles outside your COG’s true bounds are simply transparent/black pixels, but they’re still being drawn as full screen quads.  As you zoom in, fewer tiles overlap your actual image extent and the “black wedge” grows.

The fix is to *clip* the render extent to the source’s real extent.  You can do this in two places:

  1. In the **layer** constructor, default the layer’s extent to the source’s extent if none was specified.
  2. In the **renderer**’s `getRenderExtent`, also intersect against the source’s extent.

